<span class="ltx_personname">Zhi-Quan Luo<sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Shenzhen
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Shenzhen Research Institute of Big Data
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We present Fast Causal Attention (FCA), an algorithm that computes exact Causal Attention using 10% fewer operations. FCA accelerates a special class of matrix multiplications where either one operand or the output matrix is upper- or lower-triangular. This includes all operations in forward and backward pass of Causal Attention, such as masked product <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mask}(QK^{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mask</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mask}(QK^{T})</annotation></semantics></math>. For these matrix multiplications on GPU, FCA reaches noticeable accelerations over the default PyTorch implementations and Triton compiled kernels. FCA is built upon algebraic identities discovered via machine learning and combinatorial search.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right"><span id="p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-1.5pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05175v1/github-logo.svg" id="g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:15/15;" width="15" height="15"></object></span></td>
<td id="p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_ref_self">Code</span></td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="fig1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05175v1/fca.png" id="g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/208;" width="429" height="208" alt="[Uncaptioned image]">
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Transformer-based neural networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bahdanau et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luong et al., 2016</a>)</cite> have become the backbone of modern sequence modeling. Their central operation - dot-product attention couples every query with a growing prefix of keys and values. In the autoregressive (causal) setting, this incurs quadratic complexity in sequence length and occupies significant part of both training and inference at long context. While hardware-aware kernels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao et al., 2022b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanovar et al., 2025</a>)</cite> have substantially reduced memory traffic and runtime, further reducing operation count and improving hardware utilization remains an important problem. As model sizes, context windows, and inference scale grow, improving even single-digit percentages from the inner loop translates into meaningful end-to-end gains.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Prior efforts to accelerate attention largely follow two paths. One line of works uses approximation methods. These methods are commonly based on low-rank <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choromanski et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Child et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> or sparsity structures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaheer et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kitaev et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roy et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. They reduce complexity but introduce distributional mismatches that can degrade quality or require architectural and training changes. Another line of works uses exact methods. These methods often focus on I/O-blocking and fusing to minimize reads/writes, but typically leave the operation count essentially unchanged <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao et al., 2022b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanovar et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ivanov et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Other exact approaches involve distributed computing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pope et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Some attention variants (e.g. Multi-head Latent Attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2024</a>)</cite>) propose another <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="KV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">KV</annotation></semantics></math> compression approach based on latent embeddings. See a survey <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tay et al., 2022</a>)</cite> for overview of alternatives.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Inference stage consideration is becoming more central for architecture design. Recall that inference stage is separated into prefill (computation of context embedding) and decode (sequential auto-regressive token generation with KV Cache <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite>). Note that prefill stage can have attention design with Causal Mask or without Causal Mask, as in PrefixLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2018</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, researchers consider algorithm design under specific scheduling and memory transfer constraints. Indeed, one such recent proposal is Grouped-Tied Attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zadouri et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Another idea is to reduce matrix class for the purpose of improving GPU utilization without significant expressivity loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao et al., 2022a</a>)</cite>. Furthermore, even matrix multiplication is not a necessary ingredient, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ailon et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Despite significant efforts, open-source frontier models still largely rely on Group Query Attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, Sliding Window Attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Child et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and Multi-head Latent Attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, without adopting most of the sparse and low-rank alternatives.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The problem of algorithm design with hardware constraints is foundational for Machine Learning and High Performance Computing. A proper constraint formulation and further search in the algorithm space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Novikov et al., 2025</a>)</cite> may provide significant benefits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This paper introduces Fast Causal Attention (FCA), an algorithm that achieves the same outputs as causal attention while performing <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 10\%</annotation></semantics></math> fewer floating-point operations. FCA is compatible with multi-head attention, group query attention, and common training features (e.g. mixed precision, dropout, bias, sinks). The key idea is based on new algebraic refactorizations of the matrix product with triangular structure induced by causal masking. FCA reduces the constant factors of the quadratic complexity without changing numerical behavior. To the best of our knowledge, FCA is the first algorithm that reduces operation count while preserving the exact same output as the original Causal Attention. This can be especially important for edge-computing and deployment on local devices, where GPU accelerations are not accessible, while operation count can cause significant energy usage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In the following sections, we describe FCA in Algorithms 1 and 2. The expressions from these algorithms were discovered in an automated way with RL-guided Combinatorial Search <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rybin et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rybin et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. The design of FCA is also inspired by AlphaTensor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fawzi et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Fast Causal Attention</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we denote the context length as <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and the embedding dimension for each attention head as <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Given <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K,V\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K,V\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, causal attention returns <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rowSoftmax}(\operatorname{Mask}(QK^{\top}/\sqrt{d}))\,V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rowSoftmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Mask</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rowSoftmax}(\operatorname{Mask}(QK^{\top}/\sqrt{d}))\,V</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Mask}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mask</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Mask}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> zeros all strictly upper-triangular entries, and <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rowSoftmax}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>rowSoftmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rowSoftmax}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> applies row-wise softmax to the lower-triangular part of the matrix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We now present Fast Causal Attention (FCA), which proceeds in two stages: Stage I computes the lower<span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>triangular attention scores and Stage II multiplies the attention scores by the Value matrix.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>FCA Stage I: Computing the Lower<span id="S2.SS1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>Triangular Attention Scores</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We view the computation</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\mathrm{Mask}(QK^{T})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Mask</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\mathrm{Mask}(QK^{T})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">as a block matrix product</p>
