              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l53.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">53:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l53.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg2.l53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Algorithm Analysis</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we provide a more precise analysis and experimental evaluation on GPUs.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Runtime on RTX4090 GPU</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a baseline we take the standard PyTorch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paszke et al., 2019</a>)</cite> compiled torch.matmul implementation of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mask}(QK^{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mask</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mask}(QK^{T})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PV</annotation></semantics></math> (torch2.7.0+cu126 version used) with FP32 precision.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For benchmarking, we implement Fast Causal Attention in FP32 on CUDA using batched gemm to compute all products <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1},...,m_{24},h_{1},...,h_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>24</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1},...,m_{24},h_{1},...,h_{10}</annotation></semantics></math>. No advanced memory management and blocking was assumed. Code can be accessed at <span class="ltx_ref ltx_ref_self">this repository</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T1.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T1.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_r"></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">torch.matmul(Q, K.t()).tril_()</td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">FCA</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=8192,\;\;d=4096" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8192</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.727em">,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4096</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=8192,\;\;d=4096</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14.2\pm 0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>14.2</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14.2\pm 0.2</annotation></semantics></math> ms</td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18.7\pm 0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>18.7</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18.7\pm 0.2</annotation></semantics></math> ms</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=8192,\;\;d=8192" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8192</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.727em">,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8192</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=8192,\;\;d=8192</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="27.0\pm 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>27.0</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">27.0\pm 0.5</annotation></semantics></math> ms</td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22.3\pm 0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>22.3</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22.3\pm 0.3</annotation></semantics></math> ms</td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison between compiled torch kernels and Fast Causal Attention Stage I. Runtime average and std computed over <math id="S3.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="400" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>400</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">400</annotation></semantics></math> runs. FP32 precision format was used. Matrices Q and K were generated with independent random normal entries.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T2.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T2.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T2.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_r"></td>
<td id="S3.T2.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">torch.matmul(P, V)</td>
<td id="S3.T2.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">FCA</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T2.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T2.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=8192,\;\;d=4096" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8192</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.727em">,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4096</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=8192,\;\;d=4096</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T2.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10.5\pm 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10.5</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10.5\pm 0.5</annotation></semantics></math> ms</td>
<td id="S3.T2.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12.1\pm 0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12.1</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12.1\pm 0.1</annotation></semantics></math> ms</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T2.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T2.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=8192,\;\;d=8192" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8192</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.727em">,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8192</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=8192,\;\;d=8192</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T2.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20.5\pm 0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20.5</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20.5\pm 0.3</annotation></semantics></math> ms</td>
<td id="S3.T2.3.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20.8\pm 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20.8</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20.8\pm 0.5</annotation></semantics></math> ms</td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison between compiled torch kernels and Fast Causal Attention Stage II. Runtime average and std computed over <math id="S3.T2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="400" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>400</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">400</annotation></semantics></math> runs. FP32 precision format was used. Matrices P and V were generated with independent random normal entries.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Total operation count dependence on sequence length and hidden dimension</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The standard algorithm uses <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(L+1)d/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(L+1)d/2</annotation></semantics></math> multiplications and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(L+1)(d-1)/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(L+1)(d-1)/2</annotation></semantics></math> additions, with a total operation count <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(L+1)(2d-1)/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(L+1)(2d-1)/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure 1 provides comparison between operation count of Fast Causal Attention and standard algorithm. Open-source models such as gpt-oss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, Qwen3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and GLM-4.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">GLM-4.5 Team: et al., 2025</a>)</cite> are labeled with a reduction between <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05175v1/qkt.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/209;" width="405" height="209" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Reduction in number of additions and multiplications for <math id="S3.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mask}(QK^{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Mask</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mask}(QK^{T})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{rowSoftmax(S/\sqrt{d})\times V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rowSoftmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{rowSoftmax(S/\sqrt{d})\times V}</annotation></semantics></math> depending on head dim <math id="S3.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and context length <math id="S3.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Memory Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the memory analysis from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao et al., 2022b</a>)</cite>, we assume common GPU architecture with SRAM size to be <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. To obtain memory transfers estimates we need to fix a specific schedule of tile multiplications. For Algorithm 1 we assume <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> are partitioned into tiles of size <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor\frac{M}{4d}\rfloor\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor\frac{M}{4d}\rfloor\times d</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume that all intermediate sums of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> that appear in <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1},...,m_{24}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>24</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1},...,m_{24}</annotation></semantics></math> are accumulated in SRAM. Row-wise tile scheduling for computation of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> will result in a number of HBM access that depends on the non-zero elements in the left term and the right term:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{RightNonzeros}_{i}\cdot 4\frac{(L/4)^{2}(d/4)^{2}}{M}+\mathrm{LeftNonzeros}_{i}(L/4)(d/4)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>RightNonzeros</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>M</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>LeftNonzeros</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{RightNonzeros}_{i}\cdot 4\frac{(L/4)^{2}(d/4)^{2}}{M}+\mathrm{LeftNonzeros}_{i}(L/4)(d/4).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Using this expression we can summarize memory transfers between HBM and SRAM as follows.</p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{rcll}\text{Algorithm }1&amp;\text{Baseline}&amp;\text{Process}\\[6.0pt]
\frac{296}{256}\frac{(Ld)^{2}}{M}+\frac{72}{16}Ld&amp;\frac{1}{2}\frac{(Ld)^{2}}{M}+Ld&amp;\text{HBM accesses to compute matrix products }m_{i},h_{i},\\[6.0pt]
\frac{34}{16}L^{2}&amp;0&amp;\text{uploads of intermediate results from SRAM to HBM,}\\[6.0pt]
\frac{72}{16}L^{2}&amp;0&amp;\text{HBM accesses to compute }S_{i}\\[6.0pt]
\frac{1}{2}L^{2}&amp;\frac{1}{2}L^{2}&amp;\text{uploads of the final result to HBM.}\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mtext>Algorithm </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mtext>Baseline</mtext></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>Process</mtext></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>296</mn><mn>256</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>M</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>72</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>M</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>HBM accesses to compute matrix products </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>34</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>uploads of intermediate results from SRAM to HBM,</mtext></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>72</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>HBM accesses to compute </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>uploads of the final result to HBM.</mtext></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{rcll}\text{Algorithm }1&amp;\text{Baseline}&amp;\text{Process}\\[6.0pt]
\frac{296}{256}\frac{(Ld)^{2}}{M}+\frac{72}{16}Ld&amp;\frac{1}{2}\frac{(Ld)^{2}}{M}+Ld&amp;\text{HBM accesses to compute matrix products }m_{i},h_{i},\\[6.0pt]
\frac{34}{16}L^{2}&amp;0&amp;\text{uploads of intermediate results from SRAM to HBM,}\\[6.0pt]
\frac{72}{16}L^{2}&amp;0&amp;\text{HBM accesses to compute }S_{i}\\[6.0pt]
\frac{1}{2}L^{2}&amp;\frac{1}{2}L^{2}&amp;\text{uploads of the final result to HBM.}\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Note that significant part of these transfers can be overlapped (latency hiding). Although at first sight it seems that intermediate expressions <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1},...,m_{24}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>24</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1},...,m_{24}</annotation></semantics></math> require extra memory, all the computations can be stored within the output placeholder <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> as shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shitov, 2025</a>)</cite>. Therefore Algorithm 1 can be implemented without extra memory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, we analyze memory transfers of the Algorithm 2. Note that by Baseline we mean standard matrix multiplication. FlashAttention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao et al., 2022b</a>)</cite> does not store full matrix <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> in HBM, therefore this access and upload count is not applicable to it.</p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{rcll}\text{Algorithm }2&amp;\text{Baseline}&amp;\text{Process}\\[6.0pt]
\frac{296}{256}\frac{(Ld)^{2}}{M}+\frac{71}{16}L^{2}&amp;\frac{1}{2}\frac{(Ld)^{2}}{M}+L^{2}&amp;\text{HBM accesses to compute matrix products }m_{i},h_{i},\\[6.0pt]
\frac{34}{16}Ld&amp;0&amp;\text{uploads of intermediate results from SRAM to HBM,}\\[6.0pt]
\frac{68}{16}Ld&amp;0&amp;\text{HBM accesses to compute }O_{i}\\[6.0pt]
Ld&amp;Ld&amp;\text{uploads of the final result to HBM.}\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mtext>Algorithm </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mtext>Baseline</mtext></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>Process</mtext></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>296</mn><mn>256</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>M</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>71</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>M</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>HBM accesses to compute matrix products </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>34</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>uploads of intermediate results from SRAM to HBM,</mtext></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>68</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>HBM accesses to compute </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>uploads of the final result to HBM.</mtext></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{rcll}\text{Algorithm }2&amp;\text{Baseline}&amp;\text{Process}\\[6.0pt]
\frac{296}{256}\frac{(Ld)^{2}}{M}+\frac{71}{16}L^{2}&amp;\frac{1}{2}\frac{(Ld)^{2}}{M}+L^{2}&amp;\text{HBM accesses to compute matrix products }m_{i},h_{i},\\[6.0pt]
\frac{34}{16}Ld&amp;0&amp;\text{uploads of intermediate results from SRAM to HBM,}\\[6.0pt]
\frac{68}{16}Ld&amp;0&amp;\text{HBM accesses to compute }O_{i}\\[6.0pt]
Ld&amp;Ld&amp;\text{uploads of the final result to HBM.}\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Numerical error analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finite precision additions and multiplications accumulate numerical errors. These errors depend on the exact implementation of the algorithm and exact number format used at every step of the computation. Table 1 and 2 provide accumulated error comparison for BF16, FP16, and FP32 formats. Fast Causal Attention accumulates <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 2</annotation></semantics></math> times larger error.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T3" class="ltx_table">
