</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Fused kernel</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Difference with FlashAttention</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">FlashAttention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao et al., 2022b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2024</a>)</cite> achieves its GPU performance through careful joint memory management of tiles of matrices <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K,V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K,V</annotation></semantics></math>. It does not require upload of intermediate expressions such as <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\mathrm{Mask}(QK^{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Mask</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\mathrm{Mask}(QK^{T})</annotation></semantics></math> from SRAM to HBM. Instead, it computes the output <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> without materializing <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> in the memory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">From this perspective, Fast Causal Attention is a step in the opposite direction. The intermediate expressions of size <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L/4)\times(L/4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L/4)\times(L/4)</annotation></semantics></math> in FCA cannot be realized in SRAM and hence would require transfer to HBM. The intuition on the behavior of Strassen-like algorithms on GPU is as follows, the total number of tiled matrix multiplications (and FLOPs) is decreased, but each tile requires more data loaded into memory, stored in intermediate expressions, and re-used in linear combinations of intermediate expressions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It is an open question whether some Strassen-like algorithm can be applied to MHA, MLA and other forms of attention with scheduling that does not need such memory transfers and has runtime outperforming FlashAttention 2 and FlashAttention 3.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05175v1/roofline_q1.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/191;" width="238" height="191" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zadouri et al., 2025</a>)</cite> on compute and memory transfer tradeoff. Fast Causal Attention has better performance with respect to the computational roof (10% FLOPs decrease), while increasing the total I/O transfer.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we introduced Fast Causal Attention, an exact algorithm for causal attention that exploits the triangular structure of masked attention. Our approach saves about 10% of FLOPs for both forward and backward passes. Structured matrix multiplication that we proposed has applications beyond causal attention. This algorithm was discovered in a systematic way with reinforcement learning and combinatorial optimization.</p>
</div>
</section>
